1
00:00:00,591 --> 00:00:05,489
This is my feedback on Mozilla Otari, from building IssueScout on the hosted gateway

2
00:00:05,489 --> 00:00:09,170
over the hackathon. I'll cover what I built, what worked, what didn't,

3
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:09,784
and what's missing.

4
00:00:11,624 --> 00:00:16,295
What I built: IssueScout, an open-source GitHub issue-triage agent where every model

5
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:20,011
call goes through Otari. I used most of the feature set:

6
00:00:20,011 --> 00:00:23,715
open-weight models in six roles, routing policies with fallbacks,

7
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:29,795
built-in web search and web fetch, a DeepWiki MCP server, sandboxed code execution,

8
00:00:29,795 --> 00:00:33,363
workspace and per-member budgets, and per-user tagging for spend attribution.

9
00:00:35,191 --> 00:00:39,952
What worked well. One request combining web search, web fetch and an MCP server

10
00:00:39,952 --> 00:00:43,532
worked on the first try, with Otari running the whole tool loop.

11
00:00:43,532 --> 00:00:48,191
The Otari-Model, Provider, Fallback and Attempt-Count headers on every response made

12
00:00:48,191 --> 00:00:52,861
a per-call trace trivial. Routing policy names work as a drop-in model value.

13
00:00:52,861 --> 00:00:57,214
DeepSeek V4.1 Flash drove the code sandbox first time.

14
00:00:57,214 --> 00:01:01,270
And every open model, from a tiny embedder to Llama Guard to DeepSeek,

15
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:03,259
sits behind one key and one bill.

16
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:07,864
What didn't work. First, the first ten minutes.

17
00:01:07,864 --> 00:01:12,545
The hosted quickstart says api dot otari dot ai slash v1.

18
00:01:12,545 --> 00:01:18,091
That returns a 404. The working base URL is slash api slash v1.

19
00:01:18,091 --> 00:01:21,523
The error message is clear, but when your very first curl fails,

20
00:01:21,523 --> 00:01:24,739
you're tempted to go back to a provider SDK you already trust.

21
00:01:26,569 --> 00:01:31,705
Second, code execution. On Chat Completions, otari tool calls lists web searches and

22
00:01:31,705 --> 00:01:36,126
fetches, but not sandbox runs. A model can claim it ran your code without running

23
00:01:36,126 --> 00:01:38,433
anything, so I check billing to prove it.

24
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:41,558
And not every tool-capable model drives the sandbox.

25
00:01:41,558 --> 00:01:45,535
Qwen3 Coder was routed to Bedrock and failed with a toolConfig error.

26
00:01:45,535 --> 00:01:49,171
Codestral just replied with the literal text code execution.

27
00:01:49,171 --> 00:01:50,728
Both are listed as supporting tools.

28
00:01:52,603 --> 00:01:57,637
Third, guardrails. Otari serves Llama Guard 4 and Qwen3Guard as models,

29
00:01:57,637 --> 00:02:02,887
but every hosted guardrail needs a third-party vendor key, like Lakera or Patronus.

30
00:02:02,887 --> 00:02:07,376
A request-level prompt-injection guardrail came back with valid set to null and no

31
00:02:07,376 --> 00:02:11,399
explanation, and the moderations endpoint said Llama Guard wasn't found.

32
00:02:11,399 --> 00:02:15,705
So my guardrail is my own code, running Llama Guard as an ordinary chat completion.

33
00:02:17,592 --> 00:02:22,137
Fourth, credits. The twenty-dollar hackathon credit was never applied to my account.

34
00:02:22,137 --> 00:02:24,239
I only had the two-dollar welcome credit.

35
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:28,444
Gateway tools cost a cent per call, about a hundred times a small-model request,

36
00:02:28,444 --> 00:02:33,114
so a tool-heavy agent burns through two dollars fast, and I had to cap every run.

37
00:02:33,114 --> 00:02:37,467
A workspace key also can't read its own remaining budget, so the app only finds out

38
00:02:37,467 --> 00:02:38,512
from a 402.

39
00:02:40,358 --> 00:02:44,710
Smaller things. Web search and web fetch are off by default, and the error doesn't

40
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:49,096
say where to turn them on. Web fetch rejects max uses, even though web search

41
00:02:49,096 --> 00:02:54,608
accepts it. An MCP allowed-tools list with one wrong tool name silently exposed zero

42
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,551
tools, and the model confidently made up an answer.

43
00:02:57,551 --> 00:03:01,221
And the docs say routing policies only work with your own provider keys,

44
00:03:01,221 --> 00:03:04,596
while the dashboard happily built working policies from managed models.

45
00:03:06,424 --> 00:03:10,083
What's missing. Code-execution calls in otari tool calls.

46
00:03:10,083 --> 00:03:12,890
A remaining-budget header that a workspace key can read.

47
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:15,481
An open-model guardrail with no vendor key.

48
00:03:15,481 --> 00:03:19,061
Budgets per end user of my app, not just per workspace member.

49
00:03:19,061 --> 00:03:23,481
A request-level fallback list. A flag in the models list for which models reliably

50
00:03:23,481 --> 00:03:26,356
drive gateway tools. And docs that agree with each other.

51
00:03:28,191 --> 00:03:32,361
Would I keep using Otari? For agent-style apps like this, probably yes.

52
00:03:32,361 --> 00:03:38,180
Search, fetch, sandbox and MCP behind one OpenAI-compatible endpoint replaced three

53
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:41,077
integrations I'd otherwise build and pay for.

54
00:03:41,077 --> 00:03:43,316
Fix those gaps and it's a clear yes.

55
00:03:43,316 --> 00:03:43,918
Thanks for watching.
